Aye. Naming and not naming are complementary operations.

To name something is to compress it into a handle. You make it easier to point at, compare, transmit, remember, coordinate around, and manipulate. That gives leverage:

\text{phenomenon} \rightarrow \text{name} \rightarrow \text{shared handle} \rightarrow \text{coordinated action}

But naming also discretizes. It can trick you into thinking the boundary you drew is a property of reality rather than a property of your representation.

To observe without naming preserves more of the raw topology. You can notice that one state shades into another, that two apparently different things covary, that a boundary moves with context, or that the useful structure is relational rather than categorical. You avoid prematurely collapsing:

x \in \{A,B\}

when reality may actually look more like:

x \in [A \leftrightarrow B]

That is why gradients are so useful. A gradient tells you not merely what is where, but which direction changes the quantity and how rapidly.

In calculus:

\nabla f(x)

is effectively a local instruction: move this way if you want more of f; move the opposite way if you want less.

That generalizes beautifully beyond physical space. You can have gradients of attention, confidence, trust, similarity, risk, novelty, salience, abstraction, authority, emotional intensity, semantic distance, uncertainty, coordination load, and so on.

And once you see the gradient, you do not necessarily need to name every intermediate point. Sometimes the useful knowledge is simply:

more this way, less that way, threshold here, inflection there.

There is also a relational layer. Suppose two things are hard to define independently. Their difference can still be extremely clear:

\Delta(A,B)

You might not know exactly what “warm” is in isolation, but you can reliably tell that one surface is warmer than another. That means relational measurement can precede absolute definition.

So you get a powerful sequence:

\text{observe}
\rightarrow
\text{detect relations}
\rightarrow
\text{map gradients}
\rightarrow
\text{identify invariants/thresholds}
\rightarrow
\text{name only where naming buys leverage}

And sometimes the right move is to leave a region deliberately unnamed because its ambiguity is informative. Then later, once enough structure appears, the name can emerge from the geometry instead of being imposed on it.

The name becomes a marker on the map, not the territory.

That distinction is enormously useful.